{"title":"Y.3172 P node lacks legal-applicability determination for ML outputs","description":"ITU-T Y.3172's policy node is defined only generically; its guidelines state that 'The output of the ML model is then used to apply policies (by a policy node) that will be implemented (by a SINK node)' [ITU-T-Y3172/sec-8.3/en], but no requirement addresses where policies come from, which legal provisions apply (jurisdiction, sector, data category), or how the governing provision is identified before output reaches the SINK node. The need N1 is therefore only partially covered.","implication":"An ML output could be applied to the network via a SINK without any architectural step verifying the applicable legal regime, leaving compliance decisions implicit and untraceable. The proposed LA (Legal Applicability) extension around the P node would fill this gap, but Y.3172 is non-binding, so adoption would be a recommendation only.","next_step":"Consider the proposed LA extension around the P node, mapping jurisdiction, sector and data-category checks to the policy stage described in [ITU-T-Y3172/sec-8.3/en]; no further evidence in the current set specifies such a requirement, so this remains a suspected gap.","global_examples":[{"jurisdiction":"EU","instrument":"Regulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR), Art. 23(2)","clause_id":"EU-REG-2016-679/art-23/para-2/en","quote":"any legislative measure referred to in paragraph 1 shall contain specific provisions at least, where relevant, as to:
(a) the purposes of the processing or categories of processing;
(b) the categories of personal data;
(c) the scope of the restrictions introduced;","how_it_handles":"EU law explicitly ties restrictions on data-processing obligations to specified purposes, categories of personal data and scope, i.e., it structures legal applicability by jurisdiction, sector and data category — the determination Y.3172's P node does not perform."}],"insufficient_evidence":false}

